#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120906} :activity/id #uuid "2d42069d-5ae1-5bd7-8948-87caf7f08502" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049248} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121132 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a curve is defined by the following equation:\n\n$$\nx= y^3+y\n$$\n\nWe say that the equation of this curve is defined **implicitly** because instead of the usual $y = y(x),$ we have $x = x(y).$ Sometimes, it's convenient to define a curve this way. You'll meet more implicit curves later.\n\nTo graph this curve, we can start by selecting some values of $y$ and computing the corresponding $x$-values as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\ty=0 & \\quad \\longrightarrow\\quad  x=0^3+0=0 \\\\[5pt]\n\ty=1 & \\quad\\longrightarrow\\quad x=1^3+1=2  \\\\[5pt]\n\ty=2 & \\quad\\longrightarrow\\quad x=2^3+2=10 \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the points $(0,0),$ $(2,1),$ and $(10,2).$ If we plot these points and connect them with a smooth curve, we get the following graph:\n\nPlotting this curve in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/81/812db123dd422ff781472c73156a2f4d6e88a99aa3726668cd7a8b8882d6e0a8.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent to this curve at a point, we need to follow two steps.\n\n- First, we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}$ by differentiating the equation of the curve with respect to $y.$\n\n- Then, we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}$ by taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac1{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\\right)}\n$$\n\nWe'll explore some intuition behind this result at the end of the lesson, but for now, let's focus on carrying out the computation.\n\nWe start by differentiating $x=y^3+y$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take its reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative is also defined implicitly. In other words, $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}$ is a function of $y,$ not $x.$\n\nTo calculate the slope of the tangent to this curve at a point, say $(2,{\\color{blue}{1}}),$ we substitute ${\\color{blue}{y}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}$ into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(2,1)} & =\\frac{1}{3(1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(2,1)$ equals $\\dfrac14.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5b0053f2-47f3-5689-bf17-ed5cb69d36ff" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7757 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b397a95b-b908-5e5b-9f42-ed49282dc066" :step/math-academy-id 44886 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119939 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119510 :question/id #uuid "9bd80a60-0f80-5cc2-8193-86fb32c79913" :question/math-academy-id "e-12467" :question/problem "If $x=2y^2 + \\dfrac1{y^2}$, then find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =4y-2{y}^{-3} \\\\  & =4y-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{4}-2}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{y^3}{4y^4 -2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00ac6edc-908a-50be-9ef1-4b24ba597d1d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050749}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524978}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx Using dx/dy"} :step/id #uuid "af93feeb-7b8f-59ee-be5e-0da7d777ae54" :step/math-academy-id 44887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119940 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119511 :question/id #uuid "51e74bbf-6ce4-58c4-84b1-44a2f4c46ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-12468" :question/problem "If $x = y + 2y\\sqrt y$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}$ at the point $\\left(3 , 1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+2y\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+2{y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =1+3{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =1+3\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} =  \\dfrac{1}{1 + 3\\sqrt y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,1)} & =\\frac{1}{1+3\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0305491f-5ac7-5ba4-9a80-1a9a79b964f0" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332872}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx at a Point Using dx/dy"} :step/id #uuid "2480daf5-3ffe-516c-866a-b654e699d3c6" :step/math-academy-id 44890 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119941 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119512 :question/id #uuid "330c7f67-4c32-5ab7-9909-1835bba1b1f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12469" :question/problem "What is the equation of the tangent to the curve $x = 2y^2 -y + 1$ at the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}-y+1) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (2y)-1+0 \\\\  & =4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac1{4y - 1}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(2,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ff302ba-5a3f-599b-b038-7417af1dca71" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317202}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Tangent Line Using dx/dy"} :step/id #uuid "b4a418d9-3848-5c03-be63-b265c62b67cf" :step/math-academy-id 44893 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120910}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120910 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121133 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, we've made use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1}\n$$\n\nLet's now build some more intuition behind this result.\n\nConsider the graph of $x=f(y),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f11b52c2b788fcf1a9a161fa46843d42c10973545f2ff923750bcb4c50b9faca.png)\n\nThe slope of the tangent to $x=f(y)$ at $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\n$$\n\n \n\nrepresents the change in $x$ *per unit change* in $y$ at $P.$ According to our diagram, this is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{run}}{\\text{rise}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the slope of the tangent to the curve at $P$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{run}}{\\text{rise}}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result can also be proved formally using the so-called **chain rule**." :tutorial/id #uuid "2683a80b-0267-5fa3-b804-8d7b2373d85b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7758 :tutorial/title "Justification of the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "8de8290a-13e2-54eb-b279-f4e2f96955ad" :step/math-academy-id 44896}] :activity/title "Calculating dy/dx Using dx/dy" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120006}]}
